00:01:06.880 --> 00:01:09.690
I want to provide a
quick overview of what

00:01:09.690 --> 00:01:12.517
is Vault for those of you
that might not be aware,

00:01:12.517 --> 00:01:14.850
and then we'll jump into some
of the GCP specific things

00:01:14.850 --> 00:01:17.230
that we've been working on.

00:01:17.230 --> 00:01:19.740
So in order to talk
about Vault, it's

00:01:19.740 --> 00:01:23.123
important to talk
about what is a secret.

00:01:23.123 --> 00:01:24.540
When we talk about
a secret, we're

00:01:24.540 --> 00:01:26.490
specifically talking
about anything

00:01:26.490 --> 00:01:29.460
that can be used to
authorize or authenticate.

00:01:29.460 --> 00:01:33.360
So that might be a username and
password, an API token, a TLS

00:01:33.360 --> 00:01:34.920
certificate.

00:01:34.920 --> 00:01:38.220
But it also might be something
that's personally identifiable

00:01:38.220 --> 00:01:41.700
or business intelligence
data, like a social security

00:01:41.700 --> 00:01:44.790
number, a credit card, a
passport number, the API key

00:01:44.790 --> 00:01:47.100
to access your internal
analytics dashboards that

00:01:47.100 --> 00:01:51.750
give you a competitive
advantage in your market.

00:01:51.750 --> 00:01:54.000
And given this problem
and given that secrets

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:56.610
are a common attack
vector because they often

00:01:56.610 --> 00:02:01.080
have overly broad access or they
access critical systems data,

00:02:01.080 --> 00:02:04.080
we often ask questions like
how do our applications get

00:02:04.080 --> 00:02:07.260
secrets, how do
humans get secrets,

00:02:07.260 --> 00:02:10.380
how are secrets updated,
how are they revoked,

00:02:10.380 --> 00:02:13.230
who is using this secret right
now, why are they using it,

00:02:13.230 --> 00:02:16.830
when are they done with it, and
what do we do in a compromise.

00:02:16.830 --> 00:02:19.140
Because as much as we would
like to protect our secrets

00:02:19.140 --> 00:02:21.570
and make sure that no one
ever has access to them,

00:02:21.570 --> 00:02:24.540
at some point we have to accept
the reality that an attacker is

00:02:24.540 --> 00:02:27.212
going to exploit a
vulnerability in our software,

00:02:27.212 --> 00:02:28.670
or in our humans,
and they're going

00:02:28.670 --> 00:02:30.510
to get access to our secrets.

00:02:30.510 --> 00:02:32.400
So we need a plan
for rotation and we

00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:34.110
need a plan for revocation.

00:02:34.110 --> 00:02:35.490
What is the worst case scenario?

00:02:35.490 --> 00:02:37.170
How quickly can we recover?

00:02:37.170 --> 00:02:39.960
So what is our mean
time to patch a CVE?

00:02:39.960 --> 00:02:44.190
What is our mean time to
recovery in case of a leak?

00:02:44.190 --> 00:02:47.850
So Vault is an open-source
tool-- it's written in Go,

00:02:47.850 --> 00:02:49.710
so it's free and
open-source-- that

00:02:49.710 --> 00:02:53.120
is designed to answer these
questions and many more.

00:02:53.120 --> 00:02:56.040
It tries to be a single
source of secrets

00:02:56.040 --> 00:02:57.790
for your organization.

00:02:57.790 --> 00:03:03.240
It provides programmatic access
so that machines and humans can

00:03:03.240 --> 00:03:06.690
access the API, but
it also has a CLI

00:03:06.690 --> 00:03:09.240
and a UI for human access.

00:03:09.240 --> 00:03:12.180
So humans can interact
with the API or the UI,

00:03:12.180 --> 00:03:14.820
and then machines, or other
applications and services,

00:03:14.820 --> 00:03:17.850
can interact with
the API directly.

00:03:17.850 --> 00:03:20.520
And it's important to note
that it's cloud friendly.

00:03:20.520 --> 00:03:24.180
So Vault doesn't require that
you have some special hardware

00:03:24.180 --> 00:03:27.940
or some special hardware device
attached to a physical machine.

00:03:27.940 --> 00:03:32.640
This runs on native VMs and any
cloud provider, including GCP.

00:03:32.640 --> 00:03:34.770
So it's a very cloud
friendly, works very well

00:03:34.770 --> 00:03:35.760
in the cloud ecosystem.

00:03:35.760 --> 00:03:37.652
You can even run
it in a container.

00:03:40.390 --> 00:03:42.220
Vault encrypts all of
its data in transit

00:03:42.220 --> 00:03:47.410
using TLS 1.2 or most recently
1.3 for client authentication.

00:03:47.410 --> 00:03:50.770
The data is encrypted at
rest with 256-bit AES-GCM

00:03:50.770 --> 00:03:54.490
encryption, and it offers a
hierarchical key value store

00:03:54.490 --> 00:03:55.490
for storing data.

00:03:55.490 --> 00:03:58.580
So it's a path based
approach to storing data.

00:03:58.580 --> 00:04:03.118
And the data inside a
secret is a key value pair.

00:04:03.118 --> 00:04:05.410
One of the things that's
really interesting about Vault

00:04:05.410 --> 00:04:07.720
is that it offers
dynamic secrets.

00:04:07.720 --> 00:04:09.910
So in a traditional
world, let's say

00:04:09.910 --> 00:04:12.760
I need a database username and
password to connect to my MySQL

00:04:12.760 --> 00:04:13.690
instance.

00:04:13.690 --> 00:04:15.640
Well, an operator
has to provision

00:04:15.640 --> 00:04:18.519
that credential by running
create user and the SQL

00:04:18.519 --> 00:04:21.490
command, and then they take that
username and password and they

00:04:21.490 --> 00:04:24.910
put in a text file to give
it to the application.

00:04:24.910 --> 00:04:28.660
With Vault, Vault can
actually run that SQL for you

00:04:28.660 --> 00:04:31.390
and provision that
user automatically.

00:04:31.390 --> 00:04:34.720
So a human never
sees the credential,

00:04:34.720 --> 00:04:37.283
only the end application
sees the credential.

00:04:37.283 --> 00:04:38.950
And this helps you
maintain provenance--

00:04:38.950 --> 00:04:41.290
a one to one mapping
between a credential

00:04:41.290 --> 00:04:45.320
and the application or
service that's consuming it.

00:04:45.320 --> 00:04:47.680
This gives us a really
tight audit trail,

00:04:47.680 --> 00:04:51.142
and it allows us to time
bound our credentials.

00:04:51.142 --> 00:04:53.350
So instead of having a human
create a credential that

00:04:53.350 --> 00:04:55.270
lives forever, we
can have a machine

00:04:55.270 --> 00:04:57.550
create a credential
that's valid for 24 hours,

00:04:57.550 --> 00:04:59.320
or a week or a month.

00:04:59.320 --> 00:05:01.150
And by auto-expiring
these credentials,

00:05:01.150 --> 00:05:03.232
we reduce our surface
area for an attack.

00:05:03.232 --> 00:05:04.690
We reduce the number
of credentials

00:05:04.690 --> 00:05:06.570
and we reduce secret sprawl.

00:05:06.570 --> 00:05:09.070
And more importantly, we can
revoke any of those credentials

00:05:09.070 --> 00:05:11.500
at any time in the
event of a data breach.

00:05:14.120 --> 00:05:18.380
Humans or machines authenticate
to Vault by providing

00:05:18.380 --> 00:05:20.150
some type of credential.

00:05:20.150 --> 00:05:23.460
Vault then goes to these
third party databases--

00:05:23.460 --> 00:05:25.370
things like Redis,
MySQL, Postgres,

00:05:25.370 --> 00:05:28.340
or as we'll see in a
bit, Google Cloud--

00:05:28.340 --> 00:05:31.340
and dynamically generate
credentials on the fly

00:05:31.340 --> 00:05:33.470
and return them
to the requester.

00:05:33.470 --> 00:05:35.750
Each time someone
requests a credential,

00:05:35.750 --> 00:05:40.640
they get a brand new
unique credential.

00:05:40.640 --> 00:05:42.600
And Vault is pluggable.

00:05:42.600 --> 00:05:47.130
So I already mentioned
MySQL, Postgres, et cetera.

00:05:47.130 --> 00:05:50.820
Basically any SQL database,
including Oracle and MongoDB

00:05:50.820 --> 00:05:53.130
and SQL Server, are
supported for the database

00:05:53.130 --> 00:05:54.760
side of things.

00:05:54.760 --> 00:05:56.490
There's also cloud
provider support.

00:05:56.490 --> 00:05:58.770
So at Google, we have
full time engineers

00:05:58.770 --> 00:06:01.290
who are contributing
support to Vault. There's

00:06:01.290 --> 00:06:03.930
also plugins for other clouds
for your multi-cloud strategy,

00:06:03.930 --> 00:06:08.130
like AWS, or Azure,
or Oracle, or VMware.

00:06:08.130 --> 00:06:10.500
But then there are other
systems that we don't think

00:06:10.500 --> 00:06:11.790
of when we talk about secrets.

00:06:11.790 --> 00:06:13.380
But things like RabbitMQ--

00:06:13.380 --> 00:06:15.210
how do you get that
API key out there?

00:06:15.210 --> 00:06:17.080
Things like SSH access--

00:06:17.080 --> 00:06:18.990
how do you maintain
the ability for people

00:06:18.990 --> 00:06:20.950
to access into systems?

00:06:20.950 --> 00:06:23.670
Things like managing TLS
certificates-- running

00:06:23.670 --> 00:06:25.620
your own PKI infrastructure.

00:06:25.620 --> 00:06:27.990
Vault can actually act as
a certificate authority

00:06:27.990 --> 00:06:30.170
or an intermediate
certificate authority

00:06:30.170 --> 00:06:32.760
providing TLS or mutual
TLS authentications

00:06:32.760 --> 00:06:34.163
between your microservices.

00:06:34.163 --> 00:06:36.330
It obviously integrates
really well with other tools

00:06:36.330 --> 00:06:39.150
in the HashiCorp ecosystem,
and most recently you

00:06:39.150 --> 00:06:41.730
can also use it to provision
Active Directory users,

00:06:41.730 --> 00:06:44.820
as well as OpenID
Connect providers.

00:06:44.820 --> 00:06:50.540
So what are some ways that
you can use Vault with GCP?

00:06:50.540 --> 00:06:54.830
So we made a few key
investments in a few areas that

00:06:54.830 --> 00:06:57.590
allow you, as Google Cloud
customers and Google Cloud

00:06:57.590 --> 00:07:00.230
consumers, to gain the
benefits of running Vault

00:07:00.230 --> 00:07:03.830
as an open-source product on
GCP while leveraging other GCP

00:07:03.830 --> 00:07:05.400
tools and technologies.

00:07:05.400 --> 00:07:08.150
And the first one I want to
talk about is storage backends.

00:07:08.150 --> 00:07:11.920
So Vault itself is actually
a stateless service.

00:07:11.920 --> 00:07:15.350
It instead has pluggable
storage backends.

00:07:15.350 --> 00:07:17.720
Storage backend is just
where Vault puts its data.

00:07:17.720 --> 00:07:19.810
The easiest one to think
about is the file system.

00:07:19.810 --> 00:07:22.620
Vault is just going to write
its data to the file system.

00:07:22.620 --> 00:07:24.350
It also has an
in-memory implementation

00:07:24.350 --> 00:07:26.370
where it will store all
of its data in memory.

00:07:26.370 --> 00:07:28.880
And when you stop the process,
all of that data is lost.

00:07:28.880 --> 00:07:30.890
It's great for
local development.

00:07:30.890 --> 00:07:35.080
But in production, how could
you run Vault against a storage

00:07:35.080 --> 00:07:37.250
backend that's highly
available, provides

00:07:37.250 --> 00:07:40.050
high availability,
high durability,

00:07:40.050 --> 00:07:41.370
but also is low maintenance?

00:07:41.370 --> 00:07:43.820
Right, ideally you don't
want to carry a pager just

00:07:43.820 --> 00:07:45.530
for your storage backend.

00:07:45.530 --> 00:07:47.990
So we've made some key
investments on GCP.

00:07:47.990 --> 00:07:49.940
The first storage
backend, which is probably

00:07:49.940 --> 00:07:52.490
the most common
among our customers,

00:07:52.490 --> 00:07:56.030
is the Google Cloud Storage,
or the GCS storage backend.

00:07:56.030 --> 00:07:58.490
This is a GCP native
storage backend.

00:07:58.490 --> 00:08:00.770
You create a Google
Cloud Storage bucket.

00:08:00.770 --> 00:08:02.930
You grant Vault, or
a service account

00:08:02.930 --> 00:08:05.510
running Vault, permission
to read and write data

00:08:05.510 --> 00:08:07.110
from that storage bucket.

00:08:07.110 --> 00:08:10.123
That will give you three and
a half nines of availability.

00:08:10.123 --> 00:08:11.540
You don't have to
carry the pager,

00:08:11.540 --> 00:08:13.530
we carry that pager for you.

00:08:13.530 --> 00:08:16.380
And it allows you to run Vault
in high availability mode.

00:08:16.380 --> 00:08:19.010
So this allows you to run
multiple Vault instances

00:08:19.010 --> 00:08:23.000
in high availability
mode for you.

00:08:23.000 --> 00:08:26.420
Another storage backend we offer
for our enterprise customers

00:08:26.420 --> 00:08:29.300
or for customers who need a
little bit more availability is

00:08:29.300 --> 00:08:32.299
our Google Cloud
Spanner storage backend.

00:08:32.299 --> 00:08:34.520
Google Cloud Spanner
is GCP native.

00:08:34.520 --> 00:08:36.679
It gives you five
nines of availability

00:08:36.679 --> 00:08:38.539
in the highest configuration.

00:08:38.539 --> 00:08:41.090
Also supports Vaults
high availability mode.

00:08:41.090 --> 00:08:43.130
It also supports
Vault Enterprise.

00:08:43.130 --> 00:08:46.520
If you're running HashiCorps
proprietary enterprise

00:08:46.520 --> 00:08:49.788
offering, it's supported
on Spanner but not GCS

00:08:49.788 --> 00:08:51.830
because of the way that
Vault Enterprise requires

00:08:51.830 --> 00:08:54.290
a transactional interface.

00:08:54.290 --> 00:08:55.970
Again, both of these
storage backends

00:08:55.970 --> 00:08:57.343
support high availability.

00:08:57.343 --> 00:08:58.760
Both of these
storage backends are

00:08:58.760 --> 00:09:00.980
available with zero
maintenance to you,

00:09:00.980 --> 00:09:02.690
Google carries the pager.

00:09:02.690 --> 00:09:05.540
And the slides and I'm
showing you here are exactly

00:09:05.540 --> 00:09:08.300
how easy it is to add this
configuration to your Vault

00:09:08.300 --> 00:09:09.470
server config.

00:09:09.470 --> 00:09:13.355
It's just three or four lines
of configuration and the IAM

00:09:13.355 --> 00:09:15.980
authentication and now you're
using a Google Cloud supported

00:09:15.980 --> 00:09:18.940
storage backend.

00:09:18.940 --> 00:09:20.530
Both of these
storage backends were

00:09:20.530 --> 00:09:24.170
contributed by Google
engineers to Vault core.

00:09:24.170 --> 00:09:26.530
They're available in the Vault
open-source distribution.

00:09:26.530 --> 00:09:27.947
You don't need to
install a plugin

00:09:27.947 --> 00:09:29.800
or run any sidecar applications.

00:09:29.800 --> 00:09:31.720
You can download
Vault today, or really

00:09:31.720 --> 00:09:34.840
any of the recent versions of
Vault. These configurations

00:09:34.840 --> 00:09:37.150
[? stanzas ?] will
work out of the box.

00:09:37.150 --> 00:09:40.210
They also support GCP
native default application

00:09:40.210 --> 00:09:41.260
credentials.

00:09:41.260 --> 00:09:43.810
That means if you're running
Vault on a Google Compute

00:09:43.810 --> 00:09:46.680
Engine instance, a Google
Container Engine instance,

00:09:46.680 --> 00:09:49.150
or Google Kubernetes
engine instance, Cloud Run,

00:09:49.150 --> 00:09:52.490
Cloud Functions-- anything that
supports instance metadata--

00:09:52.490 --> 00:09:56.500
you can attach a service account
to that VM or that underlying

00:09:56.500 --> 00:09:58.132
runtime, and it
will automatically

00:09:58.132 --> 00:10:00.340
authenticate so you don't
have to worry about passing

00:10:00.340 --> 00:10:03.010
around a service account.

00:10:03.010 --> 00:10:05.920
So those are the
storage backends.

00:10:05.920 --> 00:10:08.500
Now let's talk about
secrets engines.

00:10:08.500 --> 00:10:12.640
So in Vault, secrets engines
are pluggable systems

00:10:12.640 --> 00:10:15.210
that allow us to extend
Vault's functionality.

00:10:15.210 --> 00:10:16.690
And there are two
secrets engines

00:10:16.690 --> 00:10:18.400
that I want to talk
to you about today

00:10:18.400 --> 00:10:21.430
that can really help accelerate
the way that you do security

00:10:21.430 --> 00:10:23.050
with GCP.

00:10:23.050 --> 00:10:26.990
The first is our
IAM secrets engine.

00:10:26.990 --> 00:10:29.950
So this is our Google Cloud
Identity and Access Management

00:10:29.950 --> 00:10:31.870
Vault secrets engine plugin.

00:10:31.870 --> 00:10:33.620
It's a mouthful.

00:10:33.620 --> 00:10:35.710
What this allows us
to do is to configure

00:10:35.710 --> 00:10:41.350
Vault to generate OAuth2
access tokens for provisioning

00:10:41.350 --> 00:10:43.880
access to Google Cloud.

00:10:43.880 --> 00:10:45.620
So what I do is
I configure Vault

00:10:45.620 --> 00:10:48.410
with a service account
and some permissions,

00:10:48.410 --> 00:10:50.090
and then I say,
hey Vault I would

00:10:50.090 --> 00:10:54.290
like you to generate
service accounts or OAuth2

00:10:54.290 --> 00:10:58.497
tokens for me whenever someone
interacts with this path.

00:10:58.497 --> 00:11:00.080
What Vault is going
to do is it's then

00:11:00.080 --> 00:11:02.960
going to make the various
API calls to Google Cloud.

00:11:02.960 --> 00:11:05.500
And it will generate
an OAuth2 access token

00:11:05.500 --> 00:11:07.250
which expires in a
certain amount of time,

00:11:07.250 --> 00:11:09.240
in this example 60 minutes.

00:11:09.240 --> 00:11:10.850
So this is an
OAuth2 access token

00:11:10.850 --> 00:11:13.160
that you can use to sign
and authenticate requests

00:11:13.160 --> 00:11:14.940
to Google Cloud.

00:11:14.940 --> 00:11:17.220
And they're
automatically cleaned up

00:11:17.220 --> 00:11:20.370
after that 60 minutes expires.

00:11:20.370 --> 00:11:22.020
Unless you write
this yourself, Vault

00:11:22.020 --> 00:11:24.570
is actually the only
way to date to have time

00:11:24.570 --> 00:11:29.050
based GCP-IAM credentials
that automatically expire.

00:11:29.050 --> 00:11:32.290
This allows you to provide
automated short-term access

00:11:32.290 --> 00:11:36.242
to your various Google resources
with very little overhead.

00:11:36.242 --> 00:11:38.200
So you imagine you have
some batch process that

00:11:38.200 --> 00:11:40.960
needs to get some information
from a cloud storage bucket,

00:11:40.960 --> 00:11:43.570
decrypt it with Cloud
KMS, run some data,

00:11:43.570 --> 00:11:45.280
and store the results somewhere.

00:11:45.280 --> 00:11:48.730
You can leverage a
secrets engine like this

00:11:48.730 --> 00:11:51.910
to generate those credentials
for you that automatically

00:11:51.910 --> 00:11:53.873
expire whenever the job expires.

00:11:53.873 --> 00:11:56.290
So you don't have to worry
about leaking those credentials

00:11:56.290 --> 00:12:00.840
or having a long running service
account with permissions.

00:12:00.840 --> 00:12:02.220
The next secrets
engine, which is

00:12:02.220 --> 00:12:03.660
one of our newest
secrets engine,

00:12:03.660 --> 00:12:06.870
is the Google Cloud
KMS secrets engine.

00:12:06.870 --> 00:12:08.760
This secrets engine
is an abstraction

00:12:08.760 --> 00:12:12.440
on top of Google Cloud KMS,
our Key Management Service.

00:12:12.440 --> 00:12:14.610
The Google Cloud
KMS secrets engine

00:12:14.610 --> 00:12:19.050
allows you to create, manage,
delete, update, encrypt,

00:12:19.050 --> 00:12:25.170
decrypt, sign, verify all kinds
of data through Vault's API.

00:12:25.170 --> 00:12:27.990
Now, you could do this by
using the Google Cloud KMS

00:12:27.990 --> 00:12:29.070
API directly.

00:12:29.070 --> 00:12:31.320
All of the functionality
that we expose in the plugin

00:12:31.320 --> 00:12:34.593
is also available via the
API or the UI directly.

00:12:34.593 --> 00:12:36.510
But we have a lot of
customers who are already

00:12:36.510 --> 00:12:38.220
heavily invested
in Vault, and they

00:12:38.220 --> 00:12:40.707
wanted to use the same
interface, the same policies,

00:12:40.707 --> 00:12:42.540
the same authentication
that they're already

00:12:42.540 --> 00:12:45.300
using for their multi-cloud
strategy or their hybrid cloud

00:12:45.300 --> 00:12:45.990
strategy.

00:12:45.990 --> 00:12:48.210
But they want to leverage
Google Cloud KMS,

00:12:48.210 --> 00:12:50.670
and this plugin is a way
that you can do that.

00:12:50.670 --> 00:12:52.140
The coolest part
about this plugin,

00:12:52.140 --> 00:12:55.020
though, is that it
actually allows you

00:12:55.020 --> 00:12:58.140
to encrypt and decrypt data
backed by a Hardware Security

00:12:58.140 --> 00:12:59.620
Module, an HSM.

00:12:59.620 --> 00:13:02.670
And this is exposed via
the Google Cloud KMS APIs.

00:13:02.670 --> 00:13:06.270
So just by reading and writing
from a JSON REST endpoint,

00:13:06.270 --> 00:13:08.820
you can actually have your
data be encrypted and decrypted

00:13:08.820 --> 00:13:12.270
by a physical hardware
security module, up to FIPS 142

00:13:12.270 --> 00:13:16.750
level three, if you have
those regulatory requirements.

00:13:16.750 --> 00:13:20.940
So again this is GCP native,
integrates with Cloud HSM,

00:13:20.940 --> 00:13:23.220
you can manage your
keys using Vault,

00:13:23.220 --> 00:13:25.500
you can use your existing
policies and configurations

00:13:25.500 --> 00:13:28.320
in Vault, and you could
encrypt, sign, verify,

00:13:28.320 --> 00:13:31.462
decrypt data using
the Vault API.

00:13:31.462 --> 00:13:33.420
So instead of just showing
you this on a slide,

00:13:33.420 --> 00:13:36.262
I figured I'd show you
what this looks like.

00:13:36.262 --> 00:13:38.220
So with that, we'll jump
over to the live demo.

00:13:42.350 --> 00:13:44.610
So I have a Vault server
here already set up.

00:13:44.610 --> 00:13:46.640
It's running on Google
Kubernetes Engine.

00:13:46.640 --> 00:13:48.223
And I'm just going
to go ahead and run

00:13:48.223 --> 00:13:54.560
Vault read GCP token dev token.

00:13:54.560 --> 00:13:56.220
And what this is going to do--

00:13:56.220 --> 00:13:58.480
in real time, this is very real.

00:13:58.480 --> 00:14:01.317
You would theoretically
be able to use that token

00:14:01.317 --> 00:14:03.150
for the next 60 minutes
if I don't revoke it

00:14:03.150 --> 00:14:04.410
by the end of this talk.

00:14:04.410 --> 00:14:06.210
This will give you
access to this project.

00:14:06.210 --> 00:14:08.127
You can't actually do
anything in the project,

00:14:08.127 --> 00:14:09.690
but this is real.

00:14:09.690 --> 00:14:13.980
Every time I run this command
I'll actually get a new Google

00:14:13.980 --> 00:14:15.433
Cloud access token.

00:14:15.433 --> 00:14:17.850
This is an access token that
I can authenticate to the API

00:14:17.850 --> 00:14:18.360
with.

00:14:18.360 --> 00:14:20.380
It is an OAuth2 bearer token.

00:14:20.380 --> 00:14:22.650
And I can give this to any
of the Google Cloud client

00:14:22.650 --> 00:14:25.350
libraries, or any service
account or any type

00:14:25.350 --> 00:14:27.210
of officially
supported tool, and it

00:14:27.210 --> 00:14:29.940
will authenticate
as this user and be

00:14:29.940 --> 00:14:34.420
able to access the APIs and the
permissions as dictated by IAM.

00:14:34.420 --> 00:14:37.820
So that's actually just how easy
it is to generate these tokens.

00:14:37.820 --> 00:14:42.590
And again, they automatically
expire after 60 minutes.

00:14:42.590 --> 00:14:45.555
I've already configured
the GCP KMS plugin,

00:14:45.555 --> 00:14:46.930
and I'm going to
save some typing

00:14:46.930 --> 00:14:49.670
by copying it to my clipboard.

00:14:49.670 --> 00:14:55.450
So here I'm using the Vault
API to encrypt and decrypt data

00:14:55.450 --> 00:14:58.480
using the Google Cloud
KMS managed keys.

00:14:58.480 --> 00:15:02.060
So all I do is I ask Vault
to encrypt some data.

00:15:02.060 --> 00:15:04.530
In this case, I gave
it the plaintext "hi",

00:15:04.530 --> 00:15:07.600
and Vault gives me back
some encrypted cipher text.

00:15:07.600 --> 00:15:09.910
From here I can also
create new keys,

00:15:09.910 --> 00:15:12.310
rotate keys, upgrade
keys, and even delete

00:15:12.310 --> 00:15:15.060
keys all using Vault's API.

00:15:15.060 --> 00:15:17.560
Under the hood, that's backed
by Google Cloud KMS and Google

00:15:17.560 --> 00:15:20.710
Cloud HSM, but it's abstracted
for you at the API layer.

00:15:26.010 --> 00:15:28.570
So all of these secrets
engines are developed by Google

00:15:28.570 --> 00:15:29.787
as plugins.

00:15:29.787 --> 00:15:31.620
So they are third party
plugins, but they're

00:15:31.620 --> 00:15:34.500
bundled automatically
in all Vault open-source

00:15:34.500 --> 00:15:35.700
distributions.

00:15:35.700 --> 00:15:38.460
So even though they're
developed third party plugins

00:15:38.460 --> 00:15:40.478
they're included in
Vault's distribution, which

00:15:40.478 --> 00:15:43.020
means you don't have to download
or install them on your own.

00:15:43.020 --> 00:15:44.853
If you download any
recent version of Vault,

00:15:44.853 --> 00:15:46.350
they'll be available for you.

00:15:46.350 --> 00:15:48.930
And as I said before, just
like the storage backends,

00:15:48.930 --> 00:15:52.200
these secrets engines support
the Google Cloud Application

00:15:52.200 --> 00:15:53.760
Default Credentials,
which means you

00:15:53.760 --> 00:15:56.135
don't have to worry about
passing around service accounts

00:15:56.135 --> 00:15:58.620
if you're on a Google managed
VM or something with instance

00:15:58.620 --> 00:16:01.420
metadata.

00:16:01.420 --> 00:16:04.190
So the last thing I want to
talk about is auth methods.

00:16:04.190 --> 00:16:07.600
So we've talked about how
you can run Vault on GCP.

00:16:07.600 --> 00:16:10.300
We've talked about how you can
provision accounts and interact

00:16:10.300 --> 00:16:11.560
with KMS.

00:16:11.560 --> 00:16:13.150
But if I'm a Google
Cloud user-- maybe

00:16:13.150 --> 00:16:15.610
I'm a G Suite user,
a Google Cloud user,

00:16:15.610 --> 00:16:17.170
I have these service
accounts-- how

00:16:17.170 --> 00:16:19.970
do my humans or my
applications authenticate

00:16:19.970 --> 00:16:22.150
to Vault using GCP?

00:16:22.150 --> 00:16:25.450
In other words, can I leverage
GCP as my source of trust

00:16:25.450 --> 00:16:28.510
for my underlying
authentication?

00:16:28.510 --> 00:16:29.280
And we can.

00:16:29.280 --> 00:16:33.130
We actually have the
Google Cloud auth method.

00:16:33.130 --> 00:16:35.080
In Vault, an auth
method is the way

00:16:35.080 --> 00:16:38.710
in which we convert human or
machine supplied information

00:16:38.710 --> 00:16:40.608
into an authentication
credential.

00:16:40.608 --> 00:16:41.900
And this is what it looks like.

00:16:41.900 --> 00:16:44.950
We can log into Vault
using the GCP method

00:16:44.950 --> 00:16:47.290
with a particular
role, a service

00:16:47.290 --> 00:16:50.320
account, and the underlying
service account credentials.

00:16:50.320 --> 00:16:52.960
Vault will authenticate this
against the Google Cloud IAM

00:16:52.960 --> 00:16:56.560
APIs, and it will give
me back a Vault session--

00:16:56.560 --> 00:16:58.612
a Vault token, if you will.

00:16:58.612 --> 00:17:00.070
And this allows me
to then interact

00:17:00.070 --> 00:17:02.710
with Vault given any
pre-configured policies

00:17:02.710 --> 00:17:05.829
that my security team might
have created in advance.

00:17:05.829 --> 00:17:07.800
So this is GCP native.

00:17:07.800 --> 00:17:10.359
It allows you to authenticate
with IAM service accounts

00:17:10.359 --> 00:17:12.730
or GCE instance metadata.

00:17:12.730 --> 00:17:14.599
So you can actually
authenticate to Vault

00:17:14.599 --> 00:17:17.109
just because you are
an instance in GCE

00:17:17.109 --> 00:17:19.599
or anything that has
instance metadata.

00:17:19.599 --> 00:17:21.563
And it has support for
Vault group aliases

00:17:21.563 --> 00:17:23.980
and policy management, which
is really important if you're

00:17:23.980 --> 00:17:26.590
running Vault Enterprise and
you want to use namespaces.

00:17:26.590 --> 00:17:28.840
So very quickly, let me show
you what this looks like.

00:17:37.230 --> 00:17:38.340
It's thinking.

00:17:38.340 --> 00:17:40.470
So it does take a
little bit longer

00:17:40.470 --> 00:17:43.560
because we have to make
a few different API

00:17:43.560 --> 00:17:45.550
calls to authenticate
the request,

00:17:45.550 --> 00:17:50.875
but what I did here is I gave
Vault my credential, as well

00:17:50.875 --> 00:17:53.000
as the service account that
I'm authenticating with

00:17:53.000 --> 00:17:55.660
and the role in which
I'm authenticating.

00:17:55.660 --> 00:17:58.480
And Vault gives me back a token.

00:17:58.480 --> 00:18:00.020
That token has some policies.

00:18:00.020 --> 00:18:02.270
So I basically get a
session with policies

00:18:02.270 --> 00:18:04.130
attached that were
configured and managed

00:18:04.130 --> 00:18:05.520
by my security team.

00:18:05.520 --> 00:18:08.240
So I just authenticate to Vault
using my existing Google Cloud

00:18:08.240 --> 00:18:11.280
credentials.

00:18:11.280 --> 00:18:14.810
So this is a great way to trust
Vault as the source of truth

00:18:14.810 --> 00:18:17.300
for secrets, but then
leverage the identity bits

00:18:17.300 --> 00:18:18.140
to Google Cloud.

00:18:21.380 --> 00:18:24.480
On Google Kubernetes Engine,
or any Kubernetes cluster,

00:18:24.480 --> 00:18:26.742
we also have the
Kubernetes auth method.

00:18:26.742 --> 00:18:28.700
Now this is not something
that Google engineers

00:18:28.700 --> 00:18:29.870
have specifically developed.

00:18:29.870 --> 00:18:31.740
We've partnered with
a number of folks,

00:18:31.740 --> 00:18:34.730
including folks at HashiCorp,
to write the Kubernetes auth

00:18:34.730 --> 00:18:35.460
method.

00:18:35.460 --> 00:18:37.630
But this is a way to
authenticate pods and services

00:18:37.630 --> 00:18:40.250
in Kubernetes
engine using Vault.

00:18:40.250 --> 00:18:42.110
This is GCP agnostic.

00:18:42.110 --> 00:18:44.420
It allows you to
restrict access via RBAC

00:18:44.420 --> 00:18:47.660
permissions and various
Kubernetes service accounts.

00:18:47.660 --> 00:18:50.420
You can restrict access
by Kubernetes namespaces.

00:18:50.420 --> 00:18:53.990
And it works great on GKE,
because GKE is just Kubernetes.

00:18:53.990 --> 00:18:57.860
But this also works if you
have GKE on-prem with Anthos.

00:18:57.860 --> 00:19:01.213
If you have your own AKS
cluster or EKS cluster,

00:19:01.213 --> 00:19:02.630
because this is
Kubernetes native,

00:19:02.630 --> 00:19:06.620
it'll work on all
of those as well.

00:19:06.620 --> 00:19:09.678
So these auth methods
are developed as plugins.

00:19:09.678 --> 00:19:11.470
Some of them are
maintained and contributed

00:19:11.470 --> 00:19:13.360
by Google engineers.

00:19:13.360 --> 00:19:15.670
They are also bundled in
the open-source distribution

00:19:15.670 --> 00:19:17.230
of Vault. So again,
you don't have

00:19:17.230 --> 00:19:18.730
to worry about installing
them yourselves.

00:19:18.730 --> 00:19:20.720
They just have a separate
development velocity

00:19:20.720 --> 00:19:22.150
than Vault core.

00:19:22.150 --> 00:19:24.670
And they help solve that
first secret problem.

00:19:24.670 --> 00:19:26.925
How do I authenticate
to Vault without hard

00:19:26.925 --> 00:19:28.300
coding our
credentials somewhere,

00:19:28.300 --> 00:19:29.530
how does all of this work?

00:19:29.530 --> 00:19:31.420
So these auth methods
help you do that

00:19:31.420 --> 00:19:34.240
by delegating trust to
either the Cloud platform

00:19:34.240 --> 00:19:36.900
or the Kubernetes API server.

00:19:36.900 --> 00:19:38.005
So I have a few links.

00:19:38.005 --> 00:19:39.880
If you're going to take
a picture of a slide,

00:19:39.880 --> 00:19:41.580
this is the one to
take a picture of.

00:19:41.580 --> 00:19:43.920
There are a few things here
that are really important.

00:19:43.920 --> 00:19:45.920
The Cloud Spanner and the
Cloud Storage backends

00:19:45.920 --> 00:19:49.450
are heavily documented, as well
as all of our secret engines,

00:19:49.450 --> 00:19:51.140
including all of
our auth methods.

00:19:51.140 --> 00:19:53.640
So everything I've talked about
here today is on this slide,

00:19:53.640 --> 00:19:55.328
except the last one.

00:19:55.328 --> 00:19:57.120
And I have one minute
left, so I'm actually

00:19:57.120 --> 00:19:58.120
going to show it to you.

00:19:58.120 --> 00:20:00.780
So the Vault fluent
deconfigurations

00:20:00.780 --> 00:20:04.710
allow us to abstract Vault
audit logs and system logs

00:20:04.710 --> 00:20:07.345
into Stackdriver in a
really friendly way.

00:20:07.345 --> 00:20:09.720
And I'm glad I have an extra
minute, because this is just

00:20:09.720 --> 00:20:11.218
really cool to me.

00:20:11.218 --> 00:20:13.010
So I'm going to go
ahead and jump over here

00:20:13.010 --> 00:20:14.593
into the Google Cloud
console and make

00:20:14.593 --> 00:20:15.832
this a little bit bigger.

00:20:15.832 --> 00:20:17.165
I've configured this in advance.

00:20:20.330 --> 00:20:21.940
I've configured this in advance.

00:20:21.940 --> 00:20:25.080
So again, I'm running Vault
on GCE VMs in this case.

00:20:25.080 --> 00:20:27.330
And you'll notice if I click
on this little drop down,

00:20:27.330 --> 00:20:29.090
I have two tags--

00:20:29.090 --> 00:20:32.490
the Vault project io audit
and Vault project io server.

00:20:32.490 --> 00:20:34.680
The audit logs are
actually Vault audit logs.

00:20:34.680 --> 00:20:38.027
Vault audits every request
and response in the system.

00:20:38.027 --> 00:20:40.110
And the server logs are
just the operational logs,

00:20:40.110 --> 00:20:42.870
what you would expect to
see from any microservice

00:20:42.870 --> 00:20:44.660
or any long running service.

00:20:44.660 --> 00:20:47.160
I'm going to go ahead and
filter by the audit logs here.

00:20:47.160 --> 00:20:49.860
Here are audit logs where--
and this is directly using that

00:20:49.860 --> 00:20:51.690
fluent deconfiguration--

00:20:51.690 --> 00:20:56.160
I have my IP address, the
alias ID of the credential,

00:20:56.160 --> 00:20:59.280
the API method I used, and
the path that I accessed.

00:20:59.280 --> 00:21:01.260
And right inside
Stackdriver, I can actually

00:21:01.260 --> 00:21:05.250
drill down into the direct
request and response headers

00:21:05.250 --> 00:21:06.570
in the JSON payload.

00:21:06.570 --> 00:21:07.710
This is structured.

00:21:07.710 --> 00:21:11.250
This is available, and I
can actually filter directly

00:21:11.250 --> 00:21:12.610
on some of this data.

00:21:12.610 --> 00:21:14.670
So if I just want to click
show matching entries,

00:21:14.670 --> 00:21:17.460
I can see all of the requests
that that particular token made

00:21:17.460 --> 00:21:19.470
right from inside Stackdriver.

00:21:19.470 --> 00:21:21.660
And this is not
Vault specific, but

00:21:21.660 --> 00:21:23.520
those fluent
deconfigurations allow

00:21:23.520 --> 00:21:25.050
the audit logs to
be parsed in a way

00:21:25.050 --> 00:21:27.778
that Stackdriver exposes
this functionality to you.

00:21:27.778 --> 00:21:29.320
That's all I have
for you here today.

00:21:29.320 --> 00:21:30.445
Thank you all so very much.

00:21:30.445 --> 00:21:32.970
I'll be available for questions
outside of the dev zone.

00:21:32.970 --> 00:21:33.470
Thank you.

00:21:33.470 --> 00:21:37.120
[MUSIC PLAYING]

